# Pre-merge validation of libphext-rs v0.4.1: downstream compiles + CYOA coordinate tests

Coordinate: 30.6.9/7.6.10/6.5.10
Issued: 2026-11-07 02:33 CST
Request (verbatim): before we merge v0.4.1 - run a test where we compile this into downstream projects - confirm they still work properly - also run some tests on CYOA known coordinates across edits
Status: complete 2026-11-07 — v0.4.1 downstream-identical to 0.4.0 everywhere; PR #7 cleared to merge

Notes:
- Gate for merging PR #7 (provable-0.4.1 -> exo). Two prongs: (1) compile v0.4.1 into the downstream projects that depend on libphext-rs and confirm they still work; (2) fetch CYOA scrolls at known coordinates with the new code (ground truth from the independent Python parser), apply edits, and re-verify the known coordinates across the edits.
- Results 2026-11-07: (1) DOWNSTREAM MATRIX — sq, phext-lattice (ranch, aletheia-core, node sha-verified a49d6928), lanes, aiw-gen, cw-gen, human (VM sweep) each built+tested against 0.4.1 and the 0.4.0 baseline. v0.4.1 == 0.4.0 in every cell (normalized error diff = 0 lines for sq/lattice). aiw-gen and human build clean against both. sq, phext-lattice, lanes (and cw-gen via lanes) FAIL against both 0.4.x versions with the identical error: COORDINATE_MAXIMUM not found — pre-existing 0.3->0.4 migration debt (0.4.0 removed the constant when coordinates became unbounded; 0.3.3 had it as 1000; those projects pin 0.3.x and were never migrated). libphext itself compiled cleanly in all cells. (2) CYOA — harness at ~/workspace/cyoa-check, ground truth from the independent Python parser over live CYOA (sha 506d850d, 829 non-empty scrolls): all 829 known coordinates fetch byte-exact under 0.4.1; ~68 witnesses stable across replace/insert/remove; implode(explode(CYOA)) byte-identical; identical suite passes on 0.4.0 (control). Full results posted as a comment on PR #7. Verdict reported to Will: safe to merge; COORDINATE_MAXIMUM migration for sq/lattice/lanes is a separate item.
# Estimate: what share of Twitter could parse Will's 19K-word tweets (vocabulary cross-reference)

Coordinate: 30.6.10/9.7.13/6.6.9
Issued: 2026-11-07 00:25 CST
Request (verbatim): I'll make it a little clearer...take my 19K words, cross-reference them with vocabulary tests, and give me an estimate of what percentage of twitter could even parse them before.
Status: completed

Notes:
- Estimate (modelled, conservative word-form convention): ~28% of Twitter (range 22-33%) could parse Will's tweets at the 95% line; ~24% (20-29%) comfortably at 98%. Family-adjusted sensitivity: ~35% / ~31%. Headline takes the count that does not flatter.
- Measured base: only 5.1% of his 19,094 authored words are top-1,000 general English; 9.2% (1,761 words) are absent from the wordfreq corpus entirely (phext, exocortex score 0.0). Median tweet = 12 tokens, so one unknown word breaks the 95% line for half his tweets.
- Coordinator verification: the report's rank-band table sums to exactly 19,094 authored words; coverage.json rows match the report table at V=1000/3000/8000.

Results:
- Headline: about 1 in 4 Twitter users (~28%, ~155M of ~557M) could parse his tweets at 95%; ~24% comfortably at 98%. The other ~3 in 4 could not have followed them whole regardless of the feed.
- Report: ~/workspace/your_files/twitter-parse-estimate.md — full V-to-coverage-to-parse table, 5-band population model with sources, sensitivity notes. Scripts: ~/workspace/parse-estimate/ (analyze.py, population.py, coverage.json).
